{"name":"Blue Square","hex":"#508a9a","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue square","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["On a trail map, an indicator of a ski slope of intermediate difficulty, steeper and narrower than a green circle, and with a gradient of 25–40%."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue_square"}],"entries":[{"title":"Square","blocks":[{"text":"In geometry, a square is a regular quadrilateral. It has four straight sides of equal length and four equal angles. Squares are special cases of rectangles, which have four equal angles, and of rhombuses, which have four equal sides. As with all rectangles, a square's angles are right angles, making adjacent sides perpendicular. The area of a square is the side length multiplied by itself, and so in algebra, multiplying a number by itself is called squaring."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Square_with_offset_tick_marks.svg/330px-Square_with_offset_tick_marks.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Square","wikidata":"Q164"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/The_square_among_a_family_of_rectangles_or_rhombuses.png/500px-The_square_among_a_family_of_rectangles_or_rhombuses.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/The_square_among_a_family_of_rectangles_or_rhombuses.png/1280px-The_square_among_a_family_of_rectangles_or_rhombuses.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/The_square_among_a_family_of_rectangles_or_rhombuses.png/500px-The_square_among_a_family_of_rectangles_or_rhombuses.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Among rectangles (top row), the square is the shape with equal sides (blue, middle). Among rhombuses (bottom row), the square is the shape with right angles (blue, middle).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_square_among_a_family_of_rectangles_or_rhombuses.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/YBC-7289-OBV-labeled.jpg/500px-YBC-7289-OBV-labeled.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/YBC-7289-OBV-labeled.jpg/1280px-YBC-7289-OBV-labeled.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/YBC-7289-OBV-labeled.jpg/500px-YBC-7289-OBV-labeled.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"YBC 7289, a Babylonian calculation of a square's diagonal from between 1800 and 1600 BCE","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AYBC-7289-OBV-labeled.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Square_symmetry_%E2%80%93_I.png/500px-Square_symmetry_